## Example 2: Matrix Multiplication (Tiled/Blocked Operation)

Matrix multiplication is more complex as it requires managing 2D indexing and often benefits from tiling/blocking, which RAJA handles more elegantly than manual CUDA thread indexing.

### 1. Original CUDA Code (Simplified)

We will use a simple, non-tiled implementation for direct comparison.

```c++
// cuda_matmul.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <iostream>

// CUDA Kernel for C = A * B
__global__ void matmulKernel(const float* A, const float* B, float* C, int M, int N, int K) {
    // Calculate the global row (row_idx) and column (col_idx) for the output C[row_idx][col_idx]
    int row_idx = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;
    int col_idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;

    if (row_idx < M && col_idx < N) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int k = 0; k < K; ++k) {
            // A is M x K, B is K x N
            sum += A[row_idx * K + k] * B[k * N + col_idx];
        }
        C[row_idx * N + col_idx] = sum;
    }
}

// ... (Host code for memory allocation and launch omitted for brevity)
```

### 2. RAJA Conversion

RAJA allows you to express the loop structure over the dimensions ($M$ and $N$) and lets the backend handle the mapping to threads/blocks.

```c++
// raja_matmul.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <raja/raja.hpp>

using namespace raja;

// Helper function to simulate the RAJA execution context
void run_raja_matmul(
    const std::vector<float>& h_A, 
    const std::vector<float>& h_B, 
    std::vector<float>& h_C, 
    int M, int N, int K) 
{
    // RAJA's parallel_for can be nested to handle 2D iteration spaces.
    // The outer loop iterates over rows (M), the inner over columns (N).

    // We use a nested parallel_for structure to map to the 2D grid launch.
    parallel_for(0, M, [&](int row_idx) {
        parallel_for(0, N, [&](int col_idx) {
            float sum = 0.0f;
            // The inner loop (K) remains sequential on the thread/work item
            for (int k = 0; k < K; ++k) {
                // Accessing data using 1D indexing, as is common in flattened arrays
                sum += h_A[row_idx * K + k] * h_B[k * N + col_idx];
            }
            // Writing the result
            h_C[row_idx * N + col_idx] = sum;
        });
    });
}

int main() {
    // Define matrix dimensions
    const int M = 128; // Rows of A, Rows of C
    const int N = 128; // Cols of B, Cols of C
    const int K = 128; // Inner dimension

