| CUDA Concept | RAJA Abstraction | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `__global__ void kernel(...)` | `parallel_for(policy, range, lambda)` | The kernel logic becomes the body of the lambda function. |
| `blockIdx.x`, `threadIdx.x` | Loop variable (`i`) | RAJA automatically maps the loop index to the thread ID within the parallel region. |
| `<<<grid, block>>>` | `policy` configuration | The grid/block configuration is handled automatically by the RAJA backend policy. |
| `cudaMalloc`/`cudaMemcpy` | Implicit/Explicit Memory Management | For simple examples, RAJA often allows you to use host vectors, relying on the policy to manage the transfer. For complex, high-performance code, you would use RAJA's explicit memory allocators (`raja::device_memory`). |
| `cudaDeviceSynchronize()` | Implicit Synchronization | RAJA operations are typically synchronous by default, or you can use explicit synchronization primitives if needed. |

