| CUDA Concept | RAJA Abstraction | How it Changes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `__global__ void kernel(...)` | `parallel_for(policy, range, lambda)` | The kernel function is replaced by a lambda function executed within a parallel loop construct. |
| `<<<grid, block>>>` | `range(start, end)` | The explicit calculation of grid/block dimensions is replaced by defining the iteration space (`range`). |
| `cudaMalloc`/`cudaMemcpy` | Implicit Memory Management | RAJA abstracts this. If you use RAJA's memory views, data transfer is often managed automatically by the backend policy. |
| Thread Indexing (`threadIdx.x`, `blockIdx.x`) | Loop Variable (`i`, `j`, etc.) | The thread index is implicitly mapped to the loop variable provided by `range()`. |
| `cudaDeviceSynchronize()` | Implicit Synchronization | RAJA often handles synchronization implicitly at the end of the `parallel_for` block, or you can use explicit synchronization primitives if needed. |

