## Example 2: Matrix Multiplication (Tiled/Blocked Operation)

Matrix multiplication is more complex because it requires managing 2D indexing and often benefits from tiling/blocking, which RAJA handles more abstractly than raw CUDA thread indexing.

### 1. Original CUDA Code (Simplified)

We will use a simple, non-tiled implementation for direct comparison.

```c++
// cuda_matmul.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <iostream>

// Kernel for C = A * B (C[i][j] = sum(A[i][k] * B[k][j]))
__global__ void matmulKernel(const float* A, const float* B, float* C, int M, int N, int K) {
    // Calculate the row (i) and column (j) of the output matrix C
    int i = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y; // Row index
    int j = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; // Column index

    if (i < M && j < N) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int k = 0; k < K; ++k) {
            // A is M x K, B is K x N
            sum += A[i * K + k] * B[k * N + j];
        }
        C[i * N + j] = sum;
    }
}

// ... (Host code for allocation and launch omitted for brevity)
```

### 2. RAJA Conversion

RAJA allows us to express the iteration space over the dimensions $(M, N)$ and then use a nested loop structure, letting RAJA manage the parallelization across the outer dimensions.

