## Example 2: Matrix Multiplication (Tiled/Blocked Operation)

Matrix multiplication is more complex as it requires managing 2D indexing and often benefits from tiling, which RAJA handles elegantly through its loop structure.

### 1. Original CUDA Code (Conceptual)

A CUDA implementation would involve complex thread block coordination, shared memory usage, and careful indexing to compute $C_{ij} = \sum_k A_{ik} B_{kj}$.

### 2. RAJA Portable Code

RAJA allows you to express the loop structure, and the backend (CUDA) handles the mapping to threads, blocks, and shared memory optimizations if the RAJA implementation supports them for that operation.

```c++
// raja_matrix_mul.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <raja/raja.hpp>

using namespace raja;

// Matrix dimensions
constexpr int M = 128;
constexpr int N_cols = 128;
constexpr int K = 128;

void run_raja_matrix_mul(
    const std::vector<float>& A, 
    const std::vector<float>& B, 
    std::vector<float>& C) 
{
    // C is M x N_cols

    // We use nested parallel_for loops to map the outer loops (i and j) 
    // to parallel execution units.

    // Outer loop (i): Iterates over rows of A and C (0 to M-1)
    parallel_for(0, M, [&](int i) {
        // Inner loop (j): Iterates over columns of B and C (0 to N_cols-1)
        parallel_for(0, N_cols, [&](int j) {
            float sum = 0.0f;

            // Innermost loop (k): The reduction/dot product (0 to K-1)
            // This loop is typically sequential within a single thread 
            // unless specialized reduction primitives are used.
            for (int k = 0; k < K; ++k) {
                // Accessing elements: A[i][k] * B[k][j]
                // Assuming row-major storage:
                float a_ik = A[i * K + k];
                float b_kj = B[k * N_cols + j];
                sum += a_ik * b_kj;
            }

            // Store result C[i][j]
            C[i * N_cols + j] = sum;
        });
    });
}

int main() {
    // Initialize matrices (M x K) and (K x N_cols)
    std::vector<float> A(M * K, 1.0f / K); // Initialize A to 1/K
    std::vector<float> B(K * N_cols, 1.0f / K); // Initialize B to 1/K
    std::vector<float> C(M * N_cols, 0.0f);

    std::cout << "Running RAJA Matrix Multiplication (M=" << M << ", N=" << N_cols << ", K=" << K << ")..." << std::endl;

    // The RAJA policy handles the mapping of the parallel_for loops 
    // onto the GPU threads.
    run_raja_matrix_mul(A, B, C);

    // Expected result for A=1/K, B=1/K: C[i][j] = Sum_{k=0}^{K-1} (1/K * 1/K) = K * (1/K^2) = 1/K
    float expected_value = 1.0f / K;

    std::cout << "Expected value: " << expected_value << std::endl;
    std::cout << "C[0][0] result: " << C[0] << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

